2026-11-18T20:42:29.6143456Z    image gate: sears_meeker_house_1815_prairie chicagology_square_on: verdict "differs", 6 difference(s) open: the pavilion: the photograph's is about 4.0 m wide (x 160-445 px) with the door in its south half; the build's is 2.2 m wide, 0.3 m in from the north corner and clear of the door, because K01 cannot cut a door through a projection; so the attic storey over it stands about 0.5-0.7 m north of the print's; the pavilion's own dentil band at the second storey's head (y 440-470 px), lower than the main cornice; the build carries the main cornice across the pavilion; the pediment's width: the print's runs 2.55 m (x 305-487 px), the build's 1.72 m, held clear of the build's 2.2 m pavilion north of the door; which front stood in 1904: the photographs are undated and show Joseph Sears's house; he sold it to Mrs. Grace Murray Meeker in March 1902 with fifteen rooms (Inter Ocean, 28 March 1902), and in 1915 it had twenty-one, 'extensively remodeled' after the purchase (Chicago Tribune, 1 May 1915), Arthur Heun's remodel (Chicagology); the 1911 sheet, drawing a one-storey projection north of the front's centre and no corner bay, is the remodelled house. Whether the remodel came before 1 July 1904 is not known (T-3156): the build follows the photographs (L-k01-1815-sears-1893); the stone shoulders either side of the attic storey (y 285-330 px), where the pavilion's 4 m block stops under the narrower attic; the deck over the curb: the print shows sky straight over the cresting's rail (y 146 px), where the build's low-pitched deck shows dark through and over its cresting (y 120-165 px); the build's curb stands about 0.2 m high on this view and about 0.8 m low on the oblique's, so the curb's height and the deck's pitch are one question across the two views
